+Explicit choices and the proof appear in Appendix~\ref{app:proof-uniform}.
+\end{proof}
+The constants establish uniform stability despite the varying energy order.
+They are conservative; matrix-dependent constants are used to assess
+numerical efficiency.
+
+\subsection{Spline space and assembly}
+\label{ssec:spline-space}\label{rem:basis-identification}
+Let $N_p$ be the cardinal spline of order $p$ (degree $p-1$),
+$h=(x_{\max}-x_{\min})/N$, and
+$\phi_{h,k}(x)=h^{-1/2}N_p((x-x_{\min})/h-k)$. The homogeneous space is
+\begin{equation}
+ V_h^0=\operatorname{span}\{\phi_{h,k}:k=0,\ldots,N-p\},
+ \qquad N_h=N-p+1.
+ \label{eq:Vh}
+\end{equation}
+For $p\ge2$, these interior-supported splines have zero extensions in
+$H^1(\R)$ and hence in $V_\mu$ for $Y/2<1$. Exact variational assembly gives
+\begin{equation}
+ (\Mh)_{kk'}=(\phi_{h,k'},\phi_{h,k})_{L^2(\Omega)},
+ \qquad
+ (\Ah(\mu))_{kk'}=a_\mu(\phi_{h,k'},\phi_{h,k}),
+ \label{eq:matrices}
+\end{equation}
+so that
+\begin{equation}
+ \Ah(\mu)
+ =-(\rf-\omega_S)D_h
+  -C\Gamma(-Y)K_M^+
+  -C\Gamma(-Y)K_G^-
+  +\lambda_0\Mh,
+ \label{eq:Ah}
+\end{equation}
+where $D_h$ is the first-derivative matrix and the fractional blocks are
+Toeplitz with the $h^{-Y}$ scaling
+\begin{equation}
+ (K_G^-)_{kk'}=h^{-Y}\Lambda_G^-(k-k';Gh),
+ \qquad
+ (K_M^+)_{kk'}=h^{-Y}\Lambda_M^+(k-k';Mh),
+ \label{eq:toeplitz-blocks}
+\end{equation}
+the tempered connection coefficients $\Lambda_G^-,\Lambda_M^+$ being given by
+absolutely convergent Fourier-symbol integrals.  The matrices $\Mh$ and $D_h$
+are banded; the Toeplitz blocks are applied by circulant embedding in
+$O(N_h\log N_h)$ operations with no compression error.  The semidiscrete
+system is
+\begin{equation}
+  \Mh\dot\cc(\tau)+\Ah(\mu)\cc(\tau)=\ff(\tau;\mu,\psi),
+  \qquad
+  f_k(\tau)=\ell^{g}(\phi_{h,k};\tau).
+  \label{eq:semidiscrete-system}
+\end{equation}
+
+
+Here $\Mh$ and $D_h$ are banded, while the fractional blocks are Toeplitz.
+Circulant embedding applies the Toeplitz blocks in
+$O(N_h\log N_h)$ operations.
+
+\begin{remark}[Assembly convention]
+\label{rem:assembly-convention}\label{lem:conformity}\label{eq:conformity}
+The exact Galerkin connection coefficient is
+\[
+ \Lambda(m;\delta)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_\R
+ |\widehat N_p(\vartheta)|^2S(\vartheta;\delta)
+ e^{\mathrm i\vartheta m}\,d\vartheta.
+\]
+The numerical solver samples the integrand at $L=2N_h$ points in
+$[-\pi,\pi)$ and uses an inverse FFT. This principal-period rule defines the
+assembled discrete system used for prediction, residuals, correction and
+benchmarking. Its difference from the real-line Galerkin integral is
+quantified in Section~\ref{ssec:refinement}. All algebraic results apply to
+these assembled matrices under the stated positive-definiteness hypotheses.
+Statements derived from the continuous form additionally require exact
+Galerkin assembly.
+\end{remark}
+
+\subsection{Prescribed coefficients and the free system}
+\label{ssec:constrained-system}
+Write $\bar M,\bar A$ for the raw $(N+p-1)$-dimensional assembled matrices,
+and let $E$ inject the $N-p+1$ free coefficients into the raw vector.
+Let $b^n$ vanish on the free entries and carry the prescribed entries on the
+boundary. Every admissible trajectory has the form
+\[
+ c^n=Ez^n+b^n,\qquad
+ M_I=E^\top\bar M E,\quad A_I=E^\top\bar A E,\quad
+ S_I=\tfrac12(A_I+A_I^\top)=E^\top\bar S E,
+ \quad \bar S=\tfrac12(\bar A+\bar A^\top).
+\]
+For the implementation, the left prescribed coefficients equal
+$K e^{-r\tau_n}\sqrt h$ and the right ones equal zero. These are
+coefficient constraints; they are not four extra PIDE equations.
+Restricting the CN equations to the free rows gives
+\begin{equation}
+ M_I\frac{z^{n+1}-z^n}{\Delta\tau}
+ +A_I\frac{z^{n+1}+z^n}{2}=f_I^{n+1/2},\qquad
+ f_I^{n+1/2}=E^\top\!\left[\bar f^{n+1/2}
+ -\bar M\frac{b^{n+1}-b^n}{\Delta\tau}
+ -\bar A\frac{b^{n+1}+b^n}{2}\right].
+ \label{eq:constrained-cn}
+\end{equation}
+Even when the stored raw forcing $\bar f$ is zero, the effective free forcing
+generally is not. For a candidate with the identical $b^n$, its residual is
+\begin{equation}
+ \rho_I^{n+1/2}=E^\top\!\left[\bar M\frac{c_\theta^{n+1}-c_\theta^n}{\Delta\tau}
+ +\bar A\frac{c_\theta^{n+1}+c_\theta^n}{2}-\bar f^{n+1/2}\right].
+ \label{eq:constrained-residual}
+\end{equation}
+Thus extracting the free rows of the raw residual is exactly equivalent to
+forming the lifted free-space residual. Boundary discrepancies must first be
+removed by projection or separately bounded; zeroing their residual entries
+alone does not control them. The error then satisfies $e^n=Ee_I^n$ and
+$(e^n)^\top\bar M e^n=(e_I^n)^\top M_I e_I^n$.
+The reliability, efficiency and inexact-Riesz results below apply directly to
+$(M_I,A_I,S_I,\rho_I)$ when $S_I\succ0$. 
+The raw basis has indices $k=-(p-1),\ldots,N-1$. Prescribing the first and
+last $p-1$ coefficients leaves exactly \eqref{eq:Vh}. At $N=64$, order
+$p=3$ therefore means quadratic splines, 66 stored coefficients and 62 free
+unknowns. Certification uses $M_I$ itself, rather than a restriction of
+$\bar M^{-1}$, and restricts all operator and preconditioner actions to the
+same free space.
+
+\subsection{Time discretisation and error target}
+\label{ssec:reference-time}\label{ssec:deterministic-error}\label{ssec:lifting-cancels}
+On the uniform grid $\tau_n=n\dt$, $\dt=T/N_T$, write
+$\overline v^{n+1/2}=(v^{n+1}+v^n)/2$. The numerical reference uses pure CN,
+without startup smoothing. In the following algebraic analysis,
+$(\Mh,\Ah,\Sh,\cc,\ff)$ denotes
+$(M_I,A_I,S_I,\zz,f_I)$; in exact-assembly statements it denotes their
+variational counterparts. Thus the reference solves
+\begin{equation}
+ \Mh\frac{\cc_h^{n+1}-\cc_h^n}{\dt}
+ +\Ah(\mu)\overline\cc_h^{\,n+1/2}
+ =\overline\ff^{\,n+1/2}.
+ \label{eq:cn-reference}
+\end{equation}
+
+For the numerical affine representation, the prescribed coefficient vector
+$b^n$ plays the role of the common lifting. Because reference and candidate
+share this lifting,
+$V_h^n-V_{\theta,h}^n=U_h^n-U_{\theta,h}^n$, and
+$\|V_h^n-V_{\theta,h}^n\|_{L^2(\Omega)}=\|\ee^n\|_{\Mh}$.
+The continuum error decomposes as
+\begin{equation}
+ V(\tau_n)-V_{\theta,h}^n
+ =[V(\tau_n)-V_h^n]+[V_h^n-V_{\theta,h}^n].
+ \label{eq:total-error-split}
+\end{equation}
+The certificate controls the second term. Under exact assembly and the
+regularity and buffer hypotheses of \cite{DamircheliCGMY2026}, the first
+admits the deterministic estimate recorded in
+Appendix~\ref{ssec:total-error}. For the principal-period discretisation,
+its size is assessed by independent prices and refinement.
+\section{Neural and reduced trajectory approximations}
+\label{sec:operator}
+\subsection{Coefficient-valued neural map}
+\label{ssec:target}\label{ssec:architectures}\label{ssec:exact-ic}
+A candidate predicts coefficients in the fixed spline space:
+\begin{equation}
+ (\mu,T,\psi,\tau)\longmapsto\cc_\theta(\tau;\mu,T,\psi),
+ \qquad U_{\theta,h}=\sum_k c_{\theta,k}\phi_{h,k}.
+ \label{eq:operator-map}
+\end{equation}
+Prescribed coefficients are overwritten at every time level before
+certification. This projection makes the returned candidate admissible in
+the same affine space as the reference. Residuals use the solver's own
+operator, and spline derivatives are available analytically.
+The numerical DeepONet uses payoff and parameter branches and a time trunk,
+with latent dimension 64 and 80,002 trainable parameters. A coefficient
+decoder supplies the spline-valued output. The experiments fix $K=100$;
+functional-input notation accommodates a payoff encoder but the benchmark
+tests a parameter map for this single strike.
+
+The initial vector is the solver's specified target: solve the raw mass
+projection $\bar M\bar\cc^0=\bar\bb^0$, with
+$\bar b_k^0=(\psi,\phi_{h,k})_{L^2(\Omega)}$, then impose the four prescribed
+coefficients. Its free part is $\zz_h^0=E^\top\cc_h^0$.
+This projection followed by overwriting generally differs from the
+constrained mass projection; all reference and training trajectories use
+the former. The neural model embeds this target as
+\begin{equation}
+ \cc_\theta(\tau)=\cc_h^0+\tau\mathcal N_\theta(\tau,\mu,T,\psi).
+ \label{eq:exact-ic}
+\end{equation}
+The initial discrepancy
+$\beta_0=\|\zz_h^0-\zz_\theta^0\|_{M_I}$ is evaluated explicitly for every
+candidate. It vanishes for the neural checkpoints and is generally nonzero
+for reduced trajectories.
+
+\subsection{Training objective and data}
+\label{ssec:losses}\label{sec:data}
+The relative mass-weighted data loss is
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathcal L_{\rm data}
+ =\frac{(\cc_\theta-\cc_h)^\top\Mh(\cc_\theta-\cc_h)}
+        {\cc_h^\top\Mh\cc_h+\epsilon},
+ \label{eq:data-loss}
+\end{equation}
+which is the relative squared $L^2(\Omega)$ error of the homogeneous
+spline field, with regularisation $\epsilon$,
+
+A compatible residual regulariser is
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathcal L_{\rm res}
+ =\frac{1}{N_T}\sum_{n=0}^{N_T-1}
+ (\rr_\theta^{n+1/2})^\top\Sh(\mu)^{-1}\rr_\theta^{n+1/2},
+ \label{eq:residual-loss}
+\end{equation}
+with $\rr_\theta^{n+1/2}$ given by \eqref{eq:cn-residual}, and the hybrid
+objective is
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathcal L=\mathcal L_{\rm data}+\lambda_R\mathcal L_{\rm res}.
+ \label{eq:hybrid-loss}
+\end{equation}
+
+For a uniform grid the time-accumulated residual satisfies
+$\etaSharp^2=T\mathcal L_{\rm res}$. It controls absolute mass error together
+with the initial term; it is distinct from the normalised data loss.
+
+The training set consists of 800 Sobol-sampled trajectories (seed 0),
+with a separate 30-query validation set (seed 999999). The spatial domain is
+$\Omega=(\log100-8,\log100+8)$, with $N=64$, order $p=3$ and 60 CN steps.
+The documented optimiser is Adam with learning rate $2\times10^{-3}$ and
+cosine scheduling; the checkpoint is the final-epoch model, without
+validation-based selection. The documented training time is 372.3 s,
+excluding trajectory generation. The original training entry script is
+absent from the supplied numerical archive, so complete retraining is not
+reproducible from this bundle alone. Evaluation uses fixed checkpoints,
+with compatibility checks for the mesh, time grid and parameter box.
+
+\subsection{Mass-weighted POD trajectories}
+\label{ssec:pod-construction}
+With $M_I=LL^\top$ and the uncentred free snapshot matrix $Z$, define
+$V=L^{-\top}U_r$, where $U_r$ contains the leading left singular vectors of
+$L^\top Z$. Then $V^\top M_IV=I$ and the subspace minimises the aggregate
+training-snapshot projection error in the mass norm. The 800 trajectories
+supply 48,800 snapshots. The reduced equations are
+\begin{equation}
+ \frac{\alpha^{n+1}-\alpha^n}{\dt}
+ + (V^\top A_I V)\frac{\alpha^{n+1}+\alpha^n}{2}
+ = V^\top f_I^{n+1/2},\qquad
+ \alpha^0=V^\top M_I \zz_h^0,
+ \label{eq:repaired-pod-cn}
+\end{equation}
+
+The reconstructed free candidate $V\alpha^n$ shares the prescribed boundary
+coefficients. Its finite-rank initial discrepancy enters the same
+certificate as the neural candidate. Both approximations are tied to the
+training mesh.
+\section{Residual certification and tolerance-controlled correction}
+\label{sec:certificate}\label{sec:pcc}
+\subsection{Energy norm and Crank--Nicolson bound}
+\label{ssec:riesz}\label{ssec:cn-certificate}
+For a fixed query, assume $\Mh\succ0$ and
+$\Sh=(\Ah+\Ah^\top)/2\succ0$ on the free space. Define
+\begin{equation}
+ \|v\|_{\Sh}^2=v^\top\Sh v,\qquad
+ \|r\|_{\Sh^{-1}}^2=r^\top\Sh^{-1}r
+ =\sup_{v\ne0}\frac{(r^\top v)^2}{v^\top\Sh v}.
+ \label{eq:Sh}
+\end{equation}
+Exact Galerkin assembly implies this positivity by
+Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform-coercivity}. For numerical assembly, positivity is
+an explicit algebraic assumption assessed query by query.
+
+For an arbitrary candidate trajectory $\{\cc_\theta^n\}_{n=0}^{N_T}$ define the
+Crank--Nicolson Galerkin residual
+\begin{equation}
+ \boxed{
+ \rr_\theta^{n+1/2}
+ :=\Mh\frac{\cc_\theta^{n+1}-\cc_\theta^n}{\dt}
+  +\Ah(\mu)\overline\cc_\theta^{\,n+1/2}
+  -\overline\ff^{\,n+1/2},}
+ \label{eq:cn-residual}
+\end{equation}
+and the coefficient error $\ee^n=\cc_h^n-\cc_\theta^n$.  Subtracting
+\eqref{eq:cn-residual} from \eqref{eq:cn-reference},
+\begin{equation}
+ \Mh\frac{\ee^{n+1}-\ee^n}{\dt}+\Ah(\mu)\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2}
+ =-\rr_\theta^{n+1/2}.
+ \label{eq:discrete-error-equation}
+\end{equation}
+
+\begin{theorem}[Crank--Nicolson residual certificate]
+\label{thm:fully-discrete-certificate}
+Let $\Mh$ and $\Sh$ be symmetric positive definite, and let reference and
+candidate use identical matrices, forcing and prescribed coefficients.
+For every $m=1,\ldots,N_T$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \|\ee^m\|_{\Mh}^2+
+ \dt\sum_{n=0}^{m-1}\|\overline\ee^{n+1/2}\|_{\Sh}^2
+ \le\|\ee^0\|_{\Mh}^2+
+ \dt\sum_{n=0}^{m-1}\|\rr_\theta^{n+1/2}\|_{\Sh^{-1}}^2.
+ \label{eq:fully-discrete-certificate}
+\end{equation}
+Consequently, with
+\begin{equation}
+ \etaSharp^2=\dt\sum_{n=0}^{N_T-1}
+ (\rr_\theta^{n+1/2})^\top\Sh^{-1}\rr_\theta^{n+1/2},
+ \qquad \eta_{\rm aug,sh}=(\beta_0^2+\etaSharp^2)^{1/2},
+ \label{eq:sharp-estimator}
+\end{equation}
+where $\beta_0\ge\|\ee^0\|_{\Mh}$, one has
+\begin{equation}
+ \max_{0\le m\le N_T}\|V_h^m-V_{\theta,h}^m\|_{L^2(\Omega)}
+ \le\eta_{\rm aug,sh}.
+ \label{eq:sharp-L2}
+\end{equation}
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Multiply \eqref{eq:discrete-error-equation} on the left by
+$(\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2})^\top$.  Because $\Mh$ is symmetric,
+\[
+ (\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2})^\top\Mh\frac{\ee^{n+1}-\ee^n}{\dt}
+ =\frac{1}{2\dt}\bigl(\norm{\ee^{n+1}}_{\Mh}^2-\norm{\ee^n}_{\Mh}^2\bigr),
+\]
+and, by the definition \eqref{eq:Sh},
+\[
+ (\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2})^\top\Ah\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2}
+ =\norm{\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2}}_{\Sh}^2 .
+\]
+The residual term obeys
+\[
+ |(\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2})^\top\rr_\theta^{n+1/2}|
+ \le\tfrac12\norm{\overline\ee^{\,n+1/2}}_{\Sh}^2
+ +\tfrac12\norm{\rr_\theta^{n+1/2}}_{\Sh^{-1}}^2 .
+\]
+Multiplying by $2\dt$ and summing over $n=0,\dots,m-1$ telescopes the mass
+terms and gives \eqref{eq:fully-discrete-certificate}.  The right-hand side increases with $m$; taking its final value and using
+shared-lifting cancellation proves \eqref{eq:sharp-L2}.
+\end{proof}
+
+With matching initial vectors, the residual estimator vanishes precisely
+when the trajectories coincide: zero residual gives the same uniquely
+solvable CN recurrence, since the symmetric part of
+$B_+=\Mh+\dt\Ah/2$ is positive definite.
+If residual evaluation uses different matrices or forcing, the required
+residual is
+\[
+ \rho_{\rm ref}=\rho_{\rm ev}
+ +(A_h-A_{\rm ev})\overline c_\theta+(f_{\rm ev}-f_{\rm ref}).
+\]
+These discrepancies require allowances in the same dual norm. A computed
+reference also carries a solver error allowance
+(Section~\ref{ssec:benchmark}).
+
+\subsection{Upper bounds from incomplete Riesz solves}
+\label{ssec:guaranteed}
+Theorem~\ref{thm:fully-discrete-certificate} contains the exact quantity
+$\rr^\top\Sh^{-1}\rr$.  In practice the Riesz problem is solved iteratively,
+so the online method needs an upper bound that remains valid before the
+iteration has converged.  The implementation first computes a
+query-wise lower-bound estimate $\alpha_{\rm lb}(\mu)$ intended to satisfy
+\begin{equation}
+  0<\alpha_{\rm lb}(\mu)\le \lambda_{\min}(\Sh(\mu)).
+  \label{eq:alpha-lb}
+\end{equation}
+The implemented spectral estimate is
+$\widehat\lambda_{\min}-\|\widehat V^\top S\widehat V-
+\operatorname{diag}(\widehat\lambda)\|_F$.
+For an exactly orthogonal $V$ and exact arithmetic this follows from Weyl's
+inequality. A computed eigenvector matrix is only approximately orthogonal,
+and the residual and its norm are themselves rounded. The floating-point calculation
+does not enclose those defects. Hence \eqref{eq:alpha-lb} remains an
+explicit mathematical hypothesis; the double-precision estimate is not a
+validated numerical proof of that hypothesis. A complete enclosure must
+account for the orthogonality defect and arithmetic errors, or use another
+verified lower-bound construction.
+
+
+For exact assembly, the mass lower bound in
+Appendix~\ref{ssec:exact-assembly} provides an alternative spectral bound.
+For the principal-period matrices the numerical spectral estimate is used;
+its comparison with high-precision eigenvalues is reported in
+Section~\ref{ssec:bracket-results}.
+\begin{lemma}[Computable two-sided bracket for the dual norm]
+\label{lem:guaranteed}
+Let $\Sh\succ0$, let $\rr\in\R^{N_h}$, and let
+$0<\alpha_{\rm lb}\le\lambda_{\min}(\Sh)$.  For an arbitrary
+$\vv\in\R^{N_h}$ set $\sres:=\rr-\Sh\vv$ and define
+\begin{equation}
+  \mathfrak l(\vv):=2\vv^\top\rr-\vv^\top\Sh\vv,
+  \qquad
+  \mathfrak u(\vv):=\mathfrak l(\vv)
+    +\frac{\norm{\sres}_2^2}{\alpha_{\rm lb}}.
+  \label{eq:riesz-bracket-def}
+\end{equation}
+Then, for every $\vv$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \boxed{
+   \mathfrak l(\vv)
+   \le \rr^\top\Sh^{-1}\rr
+   \le \mathfrak u(\vv).}
+ \label{eq:riesz-bracket}
+\end{equation}
+The bracket width is
+\begin{equation}
+  \mathfrak u(\vv)-\mathfrak l(\vv)
+  =\frac{\norm{\sres}_2^2}{\alpha_{\rm lb}},
+  \label{eq:bracket-width}
+\end{equation}
+so it closes as the Riesz residual tends to zero. The upper bracket need
+not decrease monotonically with iteration count, because the Euclidean
+residual norm need not do so.
+\end{lemma}
+
+\begin{proof}
+With $\zz=\Sh^{-1}\rr$,
+\[
+ \rr^\top\Sh^{-1}\rr-\mathfrak l(\vv)
+ = (\vv-\zz)^\top\Sh(\vv-\zz)
+ = \sres^\top\Sh^{-1}\sres\ge0.
+\]
+Since $\Sh\succeq\alpha_{\rm lb}I$,
+$\sres^\top\Sh^{-1}\sres\le
+\alpha_{\rm lb}^{-1}\norm{\sres}_2^2$, which gives the upper bound and
+\eqref{eq:bracket-width}.
+\end{proof}
+\begin{corollary}[Augmented upper certificate]
+\label{cor:guaranteed}\label{def:guaranteed}
+Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:fully-discrete-certificate} and
+$0<\alpha_{\rm lb}\le\lambda_{\min}(\Sh)$, let $v^{n+1/2}$ be any
+approximate Riesz vectors. Define
+\begin{equation}
+ \etaGuar^2=\dt\sum_{n=0}^{N_T-1}\mathfrak u(v^{n+1/2}),
+ \qquad \eta_{\rm aug}=(\beta_0^2+\etaGuar^2)^{1/2}.
+ \label{eq:guaranteed-estimator}
+\end{equation}
+Then $\max_m\|\ee^m\|_{\Mh}\le\eta_{\rm aug}$, independently of the
+iterative stopping error. As the Riesz residuals vanish,
+$\etaGuar\to\etaSharp$.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:guaranteed} at each time step and use
+Theorem~\ref{thm:fully-discrete-certificate}.
+\end{proof}
+PCG effort controls bracket sharpness. The zero iterate gives the upper
+bound $\|\rr\|_2^2/\alpha_{\rm lb}$; convergence gives the exact Riesz value.
+
+\begin{remark}[Algebraic guarantee versus validated floating-point output]
+\label{rem:arithmetic-scope}
+The theorems and brackets are exact-arithmetic statements for the
+selected numerical matrices. A validated implementation additionally needs
+outward bounds for residual evaluation, matrix actions, bracket arithmetic,
+initial projection and final accumulation. If a computed residual
+$\widehat\rho_n$ differs from the required residual by at most $d_n$ in
+$S_h^{-1}$ norm, and $u_n$ is a validated upper bound for
+$\widehat\rho_n^\top S_h^{-1}\widehat\rho_n$, then
+\[
+ \widehat\eta^2=\beta_0^2+\sum_n\Delta\tau_n
+                (\sqrt{u_n}+d_n)^2
+\]
+is valid, where $\beta_0$ bounds the initial discrepancy and accumulation
+is rounded outward. The reported experiments demonstrate observed algebraic
+non-violation in double precision; the implementation uses ordinary arithmetic rather than these
+validated enclosures.
+\end{remark}
+\subsection{Efficiency and spot-price bounds}
+\label{ssec:efficiency}\label{ssec:greeks-cert}
+For $\Sh\succ0$, define
+\begin{equation}
+ \kappa_h=\|\Sh^{-1/2}\Ah\Sh^{-1/2}\|_2\ge1,
+ \qquad
+ |||\ee|||^2=\dt\sum_n
+ \left(\|\Mh\partial\ee^{n+1/2}\|_{\Sh^{-1}}^2
+       +\|\overline\ee^{n+1/2}\|_{\Sh}^2\right),
+ \label{eq:kappa-def}
+\end{equation}
+where $\partial\ee^{n+1/2}=(\ee^{n+1}-\ee^n)/\dt$.
+\begin{theorem}[Matrix-dependent efficiency]
+\label{thm:efficiency}
+For an assembled query with $\Sh\succ0$,
+\begin{equation}
+ \etaSharp^2\le2\max\{1,\kappa_h^2\}\,|||\ee|||^2.
+ \label{eq:efficiency}
+\end{equation}
+Under exact assembly, $\kappa_h\le C_{\rm cont}/c_1$, uniformly in the
+mesh and parameter family.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+The error equation gives
+$\|\rr\|_{\Sh^{-1}}\le\|\Mh\partial\ee\|_{\Sh^{-1}}
++\kappa_h\|\overline\ee\|_{\Sh}$. Square, use
+$(a+b)^2\le2a^2+2b^2$, and sum. The exact-assembly estimate follows from
+continuity and coercivity; see Appendix~\ref{ssec:exact-assembly}.
+\end{proof}
+This efficiency norm contains a time-derivative term; it differs from the
+maximum-in-time mass error in the reliability estimate.
+For zero initial error, the error equation also yields
+$|||\ee|||^2\le(3+2\kappa_h^2)\etaSharp^2$.
+Effectivity is measured as the augmented bound divided by
+$\max_m\|\ee^m\|_{\Mh}$.
+
+For $\Phi_I(x)$ the free-basis evaluation vector, define
+\begin{equation}
+ \beta_I(x)=\sqrt{\Phi_I(x)^\top M_I^{-1}\Phi_I(x)}.
+ \label{eq:gamma-infty}
+\end{equation}
+Cauchy--Schwarz in the mass pairing gives the discrete spot-price bound
+\begin{equation}
+ |V_h^m(x)-V_{\theta,h}^m(x)|\le\beta_I(x)\eta_{\rm aug}.
+ \label{eq:greek-Linf}
+\end{equation}
+It converts a trajectory tolerance into a price tolerance at specified
+spots. Derivative bounds and continuum error separation are recorded in
+Appendix~\ref{ssec:derivatives}.
+
+\subsection{Tolerance-controlled prediction and correction}
+\label{ssec:algorithm}\label{ssec:two-stage}\label{ssec:correction}
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+\centering
+\begin{minipage}{0.96\linewidth}
+\textbf{Tolerance-checked predict--certify--correct rule.}
+\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
+\item Construct the initial target, predict the coefficient trajectory, and
+overwrite the prescribed coefficients by the specified lifting at every time
+level (the pre-projection discrepancy is kept as a diagnostic). Compute
+$\beta_0=\|\zz_\theta^0-\zz_h^0\|_{M_I}$.
+\item Check that the time grid and residual match the uniform CN scheme, and form free residuals with the same operator, stored forcing
+and lifting as the reference.
+\item Establish a positive spectral lower bound (a numerical estimate
+in the reported implementation; the soundness theorem requires a valid
+lower bound). If this fails, return status \textsc{uncertifiable} and invoke a separately implemented
+deterministic fallback if a price is required; do not report a neural certificate.
+\item Compute the augmented upper bound $\eta_{\rm aug}=(\beta_0^2+\etaGuar^2)^{1/2}$
+with a short PCG budget. If it is at most $\varepsilon$, return
+\textsc{direct accept} with its certificate.
+\item Otherwise correct by a free CN march from $\zz_h^0$, warm-started by the
+candidate, monitor linear-solve residuals, and recertify the
+corrected trajectory. If its bound passes, return \textsc{corrected accept}
+with linear-solve convergence status recorded.
+\item If correction fails or its bound does not pass, run a direct
+fallback and recertify it (\textsc{qualified fallback}), or return
+\textsc{over budget} or \textsc{numerical failure}. A certified output is returned only when the recomputed bound passes.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{minipage}
+\caption{Tolerance-checked online rule. The status distinguishes a returned
+approximation from a returned approximation certified to the requested budget.}
+\label{alg:pcc}
+\end{figure}
+
+
+The numerical screen uses ten PCG iterations, with a maximum budget of 200
+and no preconditioner. Correction uses GMRES tolerance $10^{-10}$.
+The benchmark trajectory tolerance is $\varepsilon=10^{-3}$.
+\begin{proposition}[Guaranteed acceptance]
+\label{prop:two-stage}
+Assume the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:fully-discrete-certificate},
+a valid positive spectral lower bound, exact residual evaluation, and
+$\beta_0\ge\|\ee^0\|_{\Mh}$. Define
+$\eta_{\rm aug}=(\beta_0^2+\etaGuar^2)^{1/2}$.
+If the rule in Figure~\ref{alg:pcc} returns
+\textup{\textsc{accepted}} with $\eta_{\rm aug}\le\varepsilon$, then
+\begin{equation}
+  \max_{1\le m\le N_T}\norm{V_h^m-V_{\theta,h}^m}_{L^2(\Omega)}
+  \le \eta_{\rm aug}\le\varepsilon .
+  \label{eq:soundness}
+\end{equation}
+The conclusion is independent of the PCG stopping error. The same proof
+applies to a corrected trajectory whose recomputed augmented bound passes.
+When $\ee^0=0$, one may take $\beta_0=0$ and recover the residual-only
+rule. Bounded evaluation discrepancies additionally require the allowances
+of Remark~\ref{rem:arithmetic-scope}.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+At every time step Lemma~\ref{lem:guaranteed} bounds
+$\rr^\top\Sh^{-1}\rr$ from above by the computable upper bracket used in
+$\etaGuar$.  Summing these bounds and applying
+Theorem~\ref{thm:fully-discrete-certificate}, with the initial term bounded
+by $\beta_0^2$, proves \eqref{eq:soundness}.  
+\end{proof}
+
+Let $\dd^n$ be a correction trajectory, $\cc^n_{\rm corr}=\cc_\theta^n+\dd^n$.
+The exact correction recovering the reference trajectory solves
+\begin{equation}
+  \Mh\frac{\dd^{n+1}-\dd^n}{\dt}+\Ah\overline\dd^{\,n+1/2}
+  =-\rr_\theta^{n+1/2},
+  \qquad \dd^0=\cc_h^0-\cc_\theta^0=\ee^0,
+  \label{eq:correction-equation}
+\end{equation}
+The initial correction is zero only when the candidate reproduces the
+discrete initial target. In free coordinates it is
+$\dd_I^0=\zz_h^0-\zz_\theta^0$; the prescribed coefficients already
+match after boundary projection. With $B_\pm=\Mh\pm\tfrac{\dt}{2}\Ah$,
+\begin{equation}
+  B_+\dd^{n+1}=B_-\dd^n-\dt\,\rr_\theta^{n+1/2}.
+  \label{eq:correction-step}
+\end{equation}
+Solving \eqref{eq:correction-equation} exactly is equivalent to recomputing
+the reference solution, so the practical aim is to apply a small number of
+Krylov iterations warm-started with the candidate coefficients at each
+step, while retaining the specified initial correction.
+
+
+Uniqueness of the CN recurrence implies that an exact correction recovers
+$\cc_h$. An approximate correction is accepted only after recomputing its
+augmented bound. A conditional Richardson estimate is given in
+Appendix~\ref{ssec:correction-extension}; the numerical implementation
+uses GMRES.
+
+\subsection{Cost accounting}
+\label{ssec:cost}
+Let $T_{\rm shared}$ include per-query operator assembly, forcing,
+initial projection and shared solver setup. Let $k_B$ be the average Krylov
+work per time step and $k_S$ the Riesz work. An explicit cost decomposition is
+\begin{equation}
+\begin{aligned}
+ T_{\rm ref}&=T_{\rm shared}+T_{\rm ref,setup}+T_{\rm advance},\\
+ T_{\rm accepted}&=T_{\rm shared}+T_{\rm infer}+T_{\rm residual}
+                   +T_{\alpha}+T_{\rm Riesz},\\
+ T_{\rm corrected}&=T_{\rm accepted}+T_{\rm correction}
+                         +T_{\rm recertification}.
+\end{aligned}
+\label{eq:cost-model}
+\end{equation}
+FFT iteration work is $O(k_BN_TN_h\log N_h)$ or
+$O(k_SN_TN_h\log N_h)$ only for the corresponding matrix-action phases.
+Dense assembly and spectral decomposition have different costs and are not
+covered by this count. A dense LU reference factorises $B_+$ once per query
+and reuses it over time; its cost must be measured, especially at small sizes.
+An optimised cold solve using exactly the correction machinery is necessary
+to isolate the value of neural initialisation.
+
+
+Cold timings include fresh per-query setup. Reusable-setup timings retain
+only mesh-level objects, whose one-time cost is included when amortising.
+Network training, data generation and POD construction are excluded from
+these online comparisons. For an available basis or checkpoint, the
+amortised cost of $Q$ queries is $C_0/Q+C_1$; a complete lifecycle comparison
+would also include offline costs.
+\section{Numerical evaluation}
+\label{sec:numerics}
+\subsection{Experimental design}
+The studies use the constrained system with the principal-period assembly,
+quadratic splines, 60 CN steps and the parameter box in
+Table~\ref{tab:proposed-box}, unless otherwise stated. All candidate
+coefficients are projected to the prescribed boundary values before
+residual evaluation. Positivity assessment, Riesz solves and correction
+use the same free operators. Query populations, checkpoints and configurations are fixed for each study.
+Compatibility checks and numerical verification accompany the source records.
+
+Table~\ref{tab:populations} distinguishes the study populations.
+Statistics over spots first take the maximum within each query, and
+bootstrap intervals resample queries, rather than spots or repeated timings.
+\begin{table}[ht]
+\centering\small
+\caption{Query populations; statistics refer to the indicated study only.}
+\label{tab:populations}
+\begin{tabular}{lp{0.68\linewidth}}
+\toprule
+Study & Design\\
+\midrule
+Positivity audit & 256 Sobol points, 64 box vertices and 139 selected
+full-space failure cases (459 coordinates)\\
+Riesz and initial error & 24 audit queries for brackets; 48 audit queries
+for augmented-bound tests\\
+Matched benchmark & 120 Sobol queries\\
+POD rank study & Separate 30-query validation set\\
+Price diagnostics & 30 queries, five spots $0.8K,0.9K,K,1.1K,1.2K$;
