+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+A \emph{verified} output means that its computed augmented certificate meets
+the requested tolerance for the exact returned coefficients. This is
+acceptance by the floating-point evaluation of the algebraic criterion,
+conditional on its mathematical hypotheses, as specified in
+Remark~\ref{rem:arithmetic-scope}. A delivered output or a solver convergence
+flag alone is insufficient.
+
+\subsection{Constrained positivity and certificate verification}
+\label{ssec:audit-results}\label{ssec:bracket-results}
+The free matrix $S_I$ passes the positivity test at all 459 audit
+coordinates (Table~\ref{tab:free-spd-audit}). Testing the full raw matrix
+instead rejects 154 coordinates because it includes prescribed directions.
+This comparison explains the importance of restricting the entire
+certification system to the free space. On the 305 coordinates where both
+matrices are positive, the free dual quadratic form is no larger than the
+full-space form in every test, consistently with
+Proposition~\ref{prop:full-space-conservative}. The selected cases are not a
+random sample, and the audit establishes finite-sample evidence rather than
+uniform positivity over the parameter box.
+
+\input{tables/tab_free_spd_audit}
+
+Riesz brackets were compared with 40-digit solutions for the same assembled
+matrices, spanning residual magnitudes from corrected trajectories to raw
+neural predictions. Among 11,760 comparisons, 8,065 are resolved passes
+and 3,695 are unresolved within the conditioning-based comparison
+uncertainty; no violation is detected (Table~\ref{tab:riesz}). Most
+unresolved margins arise at converged iterates. The spectral estimate lies
+below the high-precision minimum eigenvalue on all 24 tested queries.
+These comparisons check the bracket implementation, without providing an
+outward-rounded enclosure.
+
+\input{tables/tab_riesz_brackets}
+
+The initial-error tests expose a necessary part of the bound
+(Table~\ref{tab:augmented}). Exact discrete dynamics started from perturbed
+initial coefficients have roundoff-level residuals, so a residual-only
+estimator underestimates the error in all 48 cases. The augmented bound
+holds with effectivity one. It also holds for all tested neural and POD
+candidates, with median effectivities about five.
+
+\input{tables/tab_augmented}
+
+\subsection{Matched trajectory and price benchmarks}
+\label{ssec:benchmark}
+Eight methods are evaluated on the same 120 queries. The trajectory target
+is $\eta_{\rm aug}\le10^{-3}$; the terminal-price target is
+$\max_s\beta_I(x_s)\eta_{\rm aug}\le\$0.01$ at the five specified spots.
+All methods use identical matrices, forcing and boundary data.
+For comparisons against a computed reference, its allowance is added:
+\begin{equation}
+ \max_n\|\zz_\theta^n-\zz_{\rm ref}^n\|_{M_I}
+ \le\eta_{{\rm aug},\theta}+\eta_{\rm ref}.
+ \label{eq:reference-solve-allowance}
+\end{equation}
+Timings use one thread pinned to a performance core, one warmup query
+outside the test populations, and five repetitions per query in a
+reproducibly shuffled method order. Cold timings rebuild each method's
+operator cache within the timer. Reusable setup retains only mesh data,
+the free mass factor and point-evaluation functionals.
+The benchmark and scaling jobs shared the last-level cache while running
+on separate physical cores; recorded times were approximately 10\% above
+solo medians. Pairing limits but does not eliminate contention. Reported
+95\% bootstrap intervals describe variation across queries within these
+runs, rather than hardware or cross-machine uncertainty.
+
+\input{tables/tab_benchmark_trajectory}
+
+Every method returning a tolerance-checked trajectory succeeds on all
+120 queries. Direct certification of the raw DeepONet accepts none; its
+bounds range from about 2 to 13. Consequently every neural workflow query
+requires correction. Table~\ref{tab:benchmark-trajectory} shows that its
+complete cold and reusable-setup costs exceed dense LU by 18\% and 58\%,
+respectively. Neural warm starts also exceed both dense LU and unwarmed
+GMRES costs. Rank-48 POD, by contrast, passes directly on every query and
+costs 78\% of dense LU in cold mode and 30\% with reusable setup.
+At rank 32, 111 queries require correction, removing the speed advantage.
+
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=0.94\linewidth]{figures/benchmark_ratios.png}
+\caption{Trajectory benchmark: geometric-mean time ratio to free dense LU
+with query-bootstrap intervals. All plotted methods meet the target on
+120/120 queries; the raw neural candidate meets it on none.}
+\label{fig:benchmark-ratios}
+\end{figure}
+The ratios are geometric means of paired query times on common accepted
+queries. The one-time mesh setup is about 0.57 s for all methods; amortising
+it over 120 queries preserves these conclusions. Rank-48 POD has an online
+advantage beginning with the first query \emph{given an available basis}.
+This comparison excludes the cost of generating data and constructing the
+basis.
+
+The terminal-price benchmark has the same acceptance counts. Independent
+COS prices approximate the continuum target; they lie within \$0.01 of the
+stored coarse-grid price on 9 of 120 queries, with median difference
+\$0.031 and maximum \$0.31. This measures the distinct price targets, so
+COS timings are not compared under the discrete target.
+
+\subsection{POD rank and acceptance}
+\label{ssec:pod-results}
+\input{tables/tab_repaired_pod}
+On the separate 30-query validation set, mass-weighted POD at ranks 48 and
+62 passes directly on every query, whereas ranks 8, 16 and 32 require
+correction (Table~\ref{tab:repaired-pod}). At rank 32 the median initial
+error is $1.44\times10^{-4}$, below the tolerance, but the median augmented
+bound is $1.58\times10^{-3}$. The rejection therefore cannot be attributed
+to the initial term alone. Full-rank recovery agrees with a dense solve of
+the free CN system within its residual-derived allowance on all 46 tested
+queries, including validation points, vertices and selected full-space
+failure cases. A mass-orthonormalised Euclidean-SVD comparison has similar
+errors; neither basis dominates the validation maxima. Its rank-32 model
+passes directly on four queries. Mass weighting is optimal for the training
+snapshot objective, rather than for every validation trajectory.
+
+\subsection{Solver and certification costs under refinement}
+\label{ssec:scaling-results}
+\input{tables/tab_scaling}
+The cost study refines the deterministic solve and certificate, with 60 CN
+steps held fixed. The neural and POD approximations are evaluated only at
+$N=64$. Table~\ref{tab:scaling} and Figure~\ref{fig:scaling} show approximately
+linear growth in total cost up to $N=256$. Assembly and certification
+ dominate the optimised free solvers; advancement contributes below 8\%
+of their total cost, versus 33--44\% for the iterative reference.
+At $N=512$, certification grows to about 5 s and every time step reaches
+the 200-iteration PCG cap. The bracket remains an upper bound under its
+spectral hypothesis, and the computed certificate passes on all 18 timed
+runs. Efficient fine-mesh certification therefore requires cheaper spectral
+bounds and Riesz evaluation.
+
+\begin{figure}[!htbp]
+\centering
+\includegraphics[width=0.94\linewidth]{figures/scaling.png}
+\caption{Cold solver and certificate cost under spatial refinement. Left:
+median total time. Right: phase medians for free dense LU. The study uses
+30 queries for $N\le256$ and six for $N=512$, with three repetitions.}
+\label{fig:scaling}
+\end{figure}
+
+\subsection{Assembly and continuum-price diagnostics}
+\label{ssec:refinement}\label{ssec:fourier-financial}
+Table~\ref{tab:assembly} distinguishes agreement with the principal-period
+rule from agreement with the exact real-line Galerkin integral.
+Independent 40-digit quadrature of the principal-period integral gives
+entrywise differences decreasing from about $10^{-3}$ at $N=64$ to
+$10^{-5}$ at $N=512$, with virtually unchanged free positivity decisions.
+Against real-line Galerkin coefficients, the relative operator-norm
+difference is 33--41\%, concentrated at high frequencies and persisting
+under refinement. The minimum free eigenvalue changes by less than
+$9\times10^{-4}$ relatively in those tests. Thus numerical algebraic
+positivity and exact variational assembly remain distinct properties.
+
+\input{tables/tab_assembly}
+
+Independent COS and Carr--Madan prices agree to $4\times10^{-13}$ on all
+150 price-diagnostic rows at the extended settings: COS uses 8192 modes
+and range parameter 30, and Carr--Madan has cutoff cap 51,200. A 60-digit
+Carr--Madan calculation reproduces the worst-case 40-digit checks.
+This is convergence evidence for the reference prices, rather than an
+error enclosure.
+
+\input{tables/tab_refinement}
+Spatial differences approach second-order decay, and temporal differences
+at $N=1024$ decrease at second order once the coarse startup regime is
+passed (Table~\ref{tab:refinement}). The refined gap to COS levels off near
+$5\times10^{-5}$. At fixed $h=0.125$, changing domain half-width from 6 to
+12 still changes the COS gap by about $2.5\times10^{-3}$, leaving domain
+sensitivity unresolved at that resolution.
+
+\input{tables/tab_corner}
+Two short-maturity, weakly diffusive box corners have coarse-grid price
+gaps exceeding \$2, with negative values at some spots. Spatial refinement
+reduces these gaps to roughly $10^{-3}$ at $N=1024$, whereas time refinement
+alone leaves them unchanged. The initial payoff projection error also
+ decreases with mesh size. These observations are consistent with inadequate
+spatial resolution, but do not isolate the propagated contribution of
+initial projection alone. The algebraic certificate can remain valid while
+the underlying discrete price is a poor continuum approximation.
+
+\input{tables/tab_financial}
+The financial-budget study converts the trajectory bound using
+$\beta_I(x)$ (Table~\ref{tab:financial}). Raw neural predictions pass none
+of the one-, five- or ten-cent budgets. After correction all 30 queries
+pass the one-cent target, with median price bound about
+$2.2\times10^{-6}$ dollars. Rank-48 POD passes directly on every query.
+The median discrete-to-COS gap is about \$0.03, much larger than these
+surrogate bounds.
+\FloatBarrier
+\section{Interpretation and conclusion}
+\label{sec:conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}
+The residual framework turns a candidate spline trajectory into a
+query-wise bound through the governing discrete equation. Restricting to
+the free coefficient space and retaining the initial discrepancy are
+necessary for a consistent treatment of neural and reduced trajectories.
+The Riesz bracket separates validity from iterative convergence under its
+spectral lower-bound hypothesis. The deterministic discretisation supplies
+the training data, operator, exterior constraints and correction mechanism;
+certification therefore remains anchored in numerical stability.
+
+The benchmark identifies the practical distinction between prediction
+accuracy and tolerance-controlled cost. The neural model requires
+correction throughout the tested population, while rank-48 POD passes
+directly and reduces online time. Assembly and certification dominate the
+optimised free solver costs, making their reduction a prerequisite for
+larger surrogate gains. This finding applies to the tested resolution,
+parameter family and available checkpoints; it establishes neither a
+general neural speed advantage nor a lifecycle cost advantage.
+
+Discrete certification and continuum pricing accuracy require separate
+assessment. The principal-period matrix differs from exact Galerkin
+assembly, and the coarse mesh gives substantial price errors at difficult
+corners. Refinement provides empirical convergence evidence, but no
+computable continuum-price bound for this assembled scheme. Similarly,
+ordinary floating-point checks do not supply machine-enclosed guarantees;
+validated spectral bounds and arithmetic would strengthen the implemented
+certificate.
+
+The resulting method provides scheme-consistent residual certification of
+neural and reduced European-put trajectories, together with a matched
+assessment of its online value. Its next computational priorities are
+inexpensive fine-mesh spectral bounds, efficient Riesz evaluation and
+spatial resolution adequate for the required price budget. Broader payoff
+families and mesh-transfer strategies require further evaluation.
+\FloatBarrier
